### Make your first edit

Let's change something. In VS Code's Explorer sidebar, navigate to `src/routes/+page.svelte` and click to open it.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-2/vscode-page-svelte.png"
  alt="Visual Studio Code showing +page.svelte file content"
  showChrome={false}
/>

This is the homepage of your site. Find the line that says:

```svelte
let projectName = 'My Static Site';
```

Change it to something more personal, like:

```svelte
let projectName = 'Hello from CUNY';
```

Save the file with `Cmd + S` (Mac) or `Ctrl + S` (Windows).

Now look at your browser. Without refreshing, the page has automatically updated to show your new text!

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-2/localhost-edited.png"
  alt="Browser showing the updated homepage with 'Hello from CUNY'"
  chromeTitle="Hello from CUNY"
  chromeUrl="http://localhost:5173"
/>

This is called **hot module replacement**. The development server watches for changes to your files and automatically updates the browser. It makes the edit-preview cycle incredibly fast.

Try making a few more changes. Edit the paragraph text. Change the heading. Get a feel for how quickly you can see your changes reflected.

### Explore the project structure

Before we move on, let's understand what these files do. Here's a quick tour:

- **`src/routes/+page.svelte`**  This is your homepage. The `+page.svelte` file in any folder becomes a page on your site.
- **`src/routes/+layout.svelte`**  This wraps every page. It's where you'd put headers, footers, or navigation that appears on all pages.
- **`src/app.css`**  Global styles that apply to your entire site.
- **`src/app.html`**  The HTML shell that wraps everything.
- **`static/`**  A folder for static files like images. Anything here is copied directly to your built site.
- **`package.json`**  Lists your project's dependencies and scripts.
- **`svelte.config.js`**  Configuration for SvelteKit, including how to build your site.

You can ask Copilot to explain any of these files in more detail. Just open the file and ask in the chat panel, "What does this file do?"

### Stop the development server

When you're done working, you can stop the development server by clicking in the terminal and pressing `Ctrl + C`. You'll see a message confirming the server has stopped.

You can restart it anytime by running `npm run dev` again.

## Part 4: Publishing with GitHub Actions and Pages

You have a website running on your computer, but how do you share it with the world? That's where GitHub Pages comes in. It's a free hosting service that publishes static websites directly from your GitHub repository.

The magic happens through GitHub Actions  an automation system that can run tasks whenever you push code to your repository. Our template includes a pre-configured workflow that builds your site and deploys it to GitHub Pages automatically.

### Enable GitHub Pages

First, we need to tell GitHub to serve your repository as a website. Go to your repository on GitHub in your browser.

Click on **Settings** in the top navigation bar (you may need to scroll right to see it).

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-2/github-settings-tab.png"
  alt="GitHub repository page with Settings tab highlighted"
  chromeTitle="my-first-static-site"
  chromeUrl="https://github.com/yourusername/my-first-static-site"
/>

In the left sidebar, scroll down and click on **Pages**.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-2/github-pages-settings.png"
  alt="GitHub repository Settings page showing Pages option in sidebar"
  chromeTitle="Settings - Pages"
  chromeUrl="https://github.com/yourusername/my-first-static-site/settings/pages"
/>

Under **Source**, you'll see a dropdown that likely says "Deploy from a branch." Click it and select **GitHub Actions** instead.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-2/github-pages-source.png"
  alt="GitHub Pages settings showing Source dropdown with GitHub Actions selected"
  chromeTitle="Settings - Pages"
  chromeUrl="https://github.com/yourusername/my-first-static-site/settings/pages"
/>

That's it for the GitHub side. Now we need to activate the workflow in our code.

### Uncomment the GitHub Actions workflow

Return to Visual Studio Code. In the Explorer sidebar, navigate to `.github/workflows/deploy.yml` and open it.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-2/vscode-deploy-yml.png"
  alt="Visual Studio Code showing deploy.yml file with commented workflow"
  showChrome={false}
/>

You'll see that most of the file is commented out  every line starts with a `#` symbol. These comments explain what the workflow does and provide instructions for enabling it.

We need to uncomment the workflow code. Select all the lines starting from `# name: Deploy to GitHub Pages` down to the end of the file.

The easiest way to do this is:
1. Click at the start of the line `# name: Deploy to GitHub Pages`
2. Scroll down to the bottom of the file
3. Hold Shift and click after the last line
4. This selects all the workflow code

Now we need to remove the `# ` from the beginning of each line. With the text selected, press `Cmd + /` (Mac) or `Ctrl + /` (Windows). This is the toggle comment shortcut  it will remove the comment markers from all selected lines.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-2/vscode-deploy-uncommented.png"
  alt="Visual Studio Code showing deploy.yml with workflow uncommented"
  showChrome={false}
/>

Your file should now look like this, with the YAML code no longer commented:

```yaml
name: Deploy to GitHub Pages

on:
  push:
    branches: ['main']
  workflow_dispatch:

permissions:
  contents: read
  pages: write
  id-token: write

concurrency:
  group: 'pages'
  cancel-in-progress: true

jobs:
  build:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - name: Checkout
        uses: actions/checkout@v4

      - name: Setup Node
        uses: actions/setup-node@v4
        with:
          node-version: '20'
          cache: 'npm'

      - name: Install dependencies
        run: npm ci

      - name: Build
        run: npm run build
        env:
          BASE_PATH: /${{ github.event.repository.name }}

      - name: Upload artifact
        uses: actions/upload-pages-artifact@v3
        with:
          path: 'build'

  deploy:
    needs: build
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    environment:
      name: github-pages
      url: ${{ steps.deployment.outputs.page_url }}
    steps:
      - name: Deploy to GitHub Pages
        id: deployment
        uses: actions/deploy-pages@v4
```

Save the file.

### Commit and push your changes

Now let's commit these changes and push them to GitHub. Click on the **Source Control** icon in the left sidebar.

You'll see two changed files: `deploy.yml` and `+page.svelte`. Click the **+** icon next to "Changes" to stage both files.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-2/vscode-stage-changes.png"
  alt="Visual Studio Code Source Control showing staged changes"
  showChrome={false}
/>

Type a commit message like "Enable GitHub Pages deployment" and click the checkmark button to commit.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-2/vscode-commit-deploy.png"
  alt="Visual Studio Code Source Control with commit message entered"
  showChrome={false}
/>

Now click **Sync Changes** to push your commit to GitHub. You may see a popup asking to confirm  click OK.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-2/vscode-sync-changes.png"
  alt="Visual Studio Code Source Control showing Sync Changes button"
  showChrome={false}
/>

### Watch the workflow run

Go back to your repository on GitHub in your browser. Click on the **Actions** tab.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-2/github-actions-tab.png"
  alt="GitHub repository page with Actions tab highlighted"
  chromeTitle="my-first-static-site"
  chromeUrl="https://github.com/yourusername/my-first-static-site"
/>

You should see a workflow run in progress, triggered by your push. It's running through the same steps you ran locally  installing dependencies and building the site  but on GitHub's servers.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-2/github-actions-running.png"
  alt="GitHub Actions page showing workflow in progress"
  chromeTitle="Actions"
  chromeUrl="https://github.com/yourusername/my-first-static-site/actions"
/>

Click on the workflow run to see more details. You can expand each step to see its output. This is useful for debugging if something goes wrong.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-2/github-actions-details.png"
  alt="GitHub Actions workflow details showing build steps"
  chromeTitle="Deploy to GitHub Pages"
  chromeUrl="https://github.com/yourusername/my-first-static-site/actions/runs/123456789"
/>

After a minute or two, both the "build" and "deploy" jobs should complete with green checkmarks.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-2/github-actions-complete.png"
  alt="GitHub Actions page showing completed workflow with green checkmarks"
  chromeTitle="Actions"
  chromeUrl="https://github.com/yourusername/my-first-static-site/actions"
/>

### View your published site

Your site is now live on the internet! Click on the **deploy** job to expand it, and you'll see a link to your published site.

You can also find the URL by going back to **Settings > Pages**. The URL will be in the format:

```
https://yourusername.github.io/my-first-static-site/
```

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-2/github-pages-live.png"
  alt="GitHub Pages settings showing the live site URL"
  chromeTitle="Settings - Pages"
  chromeUrl="https://github.com/yourusername/my-first-static-site/settings/pages"
/>

Click the link to visit your site. You should see the same page you were editing locally, now available to anyone on the internet.

<Screenshot
  src="/screenshots/week-2/live-site.png"
  alt="Browser showing the live published site on GitHub Pages"
  chromeTitle="Hello from CUNY"
  chromeUrl="https://yourusername.github.io/my-first-static-site/"
/>

From now on, every time you push changes to your repository, GitHub Actions will automatically rebuild and redeploy your site. The whole process takes just a minute or two.

### The deployment workflow explained

Let's take a moment to understand what the workflow file does:

- **`on: push: branches: ['main']`**  Triggers the workflow whenever you push to the main branch
- **`npm ci`**  Installs dependencies (like `npm install`, but faster and stricter for automated builds)
- **`npm run build`**  Builds your site into static HTML, CSS, and JavaScript files
- **`BASE_PATH`**  Sets the correct path for your site's assets (since it's not at the root of a domain)
- **`upload-pages-artifact`**  Uploads the built files to GitHub
- **`deploy-pages`**  Publishes those files to GitHub Pages

This workflow is doing automatically what you did manually when running `npm install` and `npm run dev`  but it also runs `npm run build` to create optimized production files, and deploys them to the web.

